{0 Rocq Frontend Boundary}

The current trusted implementation boundary is the elaborated Kairos core
language, not the extended surface language.

{[
Kx_surface_syntax.source
  -- Kx_elaborate.elaborate_source -->
Kx_ast.program
  -- Kairos_to_model -->
Verification_model.node_model
  -- verification / Why3 backend -->
proof obligations
]}

The existing Rocq development should be compared against the desugared core
program and the obligation generation that follows. Surface constructs such
as enum-indexed quantifiers, named actions, observers, history definitions,
and compact assignment notation are frontend elaborations.

Surface syntax must not be added to the Rocq core. If a surface construct is
only syntactic sugar, the Rocq-facing object is the elaborated core object,
not the original surface phrase.

{1 What This Means}

For the current implementation:

- local predicates are not a proof-kernel feature; they are expanded before the
  core pipeline;
- observers are not operational program state; they elaborate to generated
  ghost variables and ghost-only updates, with no influence on observable
  behavior;
- [history def] declarations and [history name(x)] applications are frontend
  constructs; they elaborate to reserved generated ghosts, ghost-only updates,
  and phase-specific elaboration checks before the verification model is
  built. Bounded [pre_k(self,k)] in a history update is implemented by reserved
  generated delay/snapshot ghosts;
- high-level safety schemas such as [maintainedUntil] should be ordinary
  [spec def] declarations or explicit LTL formulas using [G], [X] and [W],
  not proof-kernel features;
- pure functions and enum literals are core constructs and must be accounted
  for when lowering to Why3 or to any formal semantic model.

{1 Historical Initialization Frontier}

Historical reads are not just surface syntax. A source formula mentioning
[pre] or [pre_k] is meaningful only at a point where enough completed
instants are available. The Rocq development records this principle in two
separate layers:

- [HistoricalInitialization] defines the required historical depth of a
  formula and proves that formulas initialized at age [n] are insensitive to
  deeper, unproduced historical slots;
- [InitializationFrontier] abstracts a source-point frontier with the contract
  [frontier_available p alpha -> hformula_initialized_at (frontier_age p)
  alpha].

The OCaml implementation witnesses this contract at the source/frontend
boundary:

- [Historical_initialization.required_depth_hexpr] and
  [Historical_initialization.required_depth_ltl] compute the source-side
  required depth;
- [Historical_initialization.min_ticks_by_state] computes a control-flow
  lower bound on the number of completed instants available at each control
  state;
- [Kairos_to_model_node_validation.validate_node] rejects a formula when its
  required depth is greater than the age available at the point where the
  formula is placed.

The point-to-age convention is:

- global temporal contracts are checked at trace start, with [X] decreasing
  the required depth before the initial age-[0] check;
- state invariants are checked against the minimum tick count of their control
  state;
- transition-body assertions and while invariants are checked against the age
  of the source control state;
- transition elaboration checks are checked at the destination of the step,
  with one additional completed instant available.

This is an architectural contract, not a certification of the OCaml graph
algorithm. Rocq proves the mathematical meaning of an initialization frontier;
the implementation must keep [min_ticks_by_state] and the validator aligned
with that frontier. Tests can catch representative invalid uses, but they do
not replace a future elaboration/adequacy theorem. Unreachable control states
currently have no finite age obligation in the frontend check; if later
semantics make such points reachable, the frontier computation must be
revisited rather than treated as a Rocq theorem.

{1 Boundary Obligation}

The Rocq formalization should not include the surface language merely to
justify syntactic sugar. The obligation is instead architectural:

- the implementation must expose the desugared core program used as the
  Rocq-facing entry point;
- historical formulas accepted at each source point must satisfy the
  [InitializationFrontier] age contract before they are interpreted using
  total historical stores;
- sugar-generated proof checks must be classified separately from the
  canonical Rocq obligation families;
- tests may validate surface elaboration, but the Rocq theorem is stated for
  the core program after elaboration.

An optional, separate elaboration theorem could relate a future formal
surface semantics to the core semantics:

{[
surface_program P
  elaborates_to C
  sem_surface P traces
  <-> sem_core C traces
]}

That theorem is not part of the current Rocq core and should not drive the
paper mechanization. For the current claim, Rocq treats the elaborated core
program as the formal entry point.
